Production Deployment Guide: Self-Hosted LLM on Windows & WSL2 (Final Version)
1. Objective

This document outlines the complete, end-to-end procedure for deploying a self-hosted LLM web application on a Windows machine. The architecture uses Ollama on the Windows host and containerizes all supporting services (Tika, Streamlit) within a WSL2 Ubuntu instance. This guide incorporates critical troubleshooting steps and permanent fixes for known networking and tooling issues to ensure a stable, persistent, and automated deployment.

2. Final Architecture

Windows Host: Runs the Ollama service. Its LAN IP (e.g., 192.168.87.64) is used for communication.

WSL2 (Ubuntu 24.04): Hosts all supporting services within Docker containers for portability and isolation.

Docker Engine: Manages all containerized services within WSL.

Tika Container: Provides document parsing. Managed by a systemd service for auto-start.

Streamlit (kiosk-app) Container: The web application front-end. Managed by docker-compose.

Cloudflare Tunnel: (Optional, for public access) Provides a secure tunnel to the Streamlit app without opening firewall ports.

3. Windows Host Preparation
3.1. Install WSL2 and Ubuntu

On a fresh Windows machine, install WSL2 from an Administrator PowerShell prompt. This is the modern, recommended method.

wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04


Reboot when prompted. Launch "Ubuntu 24.04 LTS" from the Start Menu to complete the initial user setup.

3.2. Install and Configure Ollama

Download and install Ollama for Windows from the official website.

Crucially, configure Ollama to accept network connections. This is not the default behavior.

Search for "Edit the system environment variables" in the Start Menu.

Click the "Environment Variables..." button.

In the "System variables" section, click "New...".

Variable name: OLLAMA_HOST

Variable value: 0.0.0.0

Click OK to save.

Restart the Ollama service by right-clicking its icon in the System Tray and selecting "Restart Ollama," or by rebooting your computer.

Pull your desired model (e.g., ollama pull g27).

3.3. Configure Windows Firewall

Open "Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security".

Click "Inbound Rules" -> "New Rule...".

Select Port, then TCP, and specify port 11434.

Select Allow the connection.

Ensure only the Private profile is checked. This is critical for security.

Name the rule "Allow WSL to Ollama" and click Finish.

