7. Final Verification & Troubleshooting Flowchart

If you encounter issues on the new machine, follow this diagnostic flow:

Can WSL connect to Ollama?

Test: From the Ubuntu terminal, run curl http://<YOUR_WINDOWS_LAN_IP>:11434.

Expected: Ollama is running

If Fails (Timeout): Check your Windows Firewall rule. Ensure it's enabled and on the "Private" profile.

If Fails (Connection Refused): Check that the OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 environment variable is set correctly on Windows and that the Ollama service has been restarted.

Can Docker containers reach the internet?

Test: docker run --rm alpine nslookup google.com

Expected: A successful DNS lookup.

If Fails: Your system has the DNS issue. Follow the steps in section 4.2 to apply the permanent fix.

Are the containers running?

Test: docker ps

Expected: See both tika-server and kiosk-app with status Up.

If kiosk-app fails to start: Check its logs with docker logs kiosk-app.

Is the app working?

Access http://localhost:8501 in your Windows browser. If you see "LLM backend offline," it confirms the app can't reach the LLM_BASE_URL defined in docker-compose.yml. Double-check the IP address.

System Documentation Addendum: Public Access via Cloudflare Tunnel
8. Objective
This section details the configuration of a secure Cloudflare Tunnel to expose the self-hosted Streamlit application to the public internet. This method provides robust, encrypted, and authenticated access without requiring any inbound firewall rules or port forwarding on the host machine's network.
9. Component Overview
cloudflared Client: The tunnel software running as a systemd service inside the WSL2 Ubuntu instance.
Cloudflare Access: The Zero Trust policy that requires email-based authentication before allowing any user to reach the application.
Cloudflare DNS: Manages the public DNS record (www.tunalen.com) and points it to the secure tunnel.
10. Configuration Steps
10.1. Tunnel Creation and Authentication
Installation: The cloudflared client was installed in the WSL2 instance via its Debian package.
Authentication: The client was authenticated to the Cloudflare account via cloudflared tunnel login. This generated a cert.pem file in ~/.cloudflared/, authorizing the client.
Tunnel Creation: A persistent, named tunnel was created using the command: cloudflared tunnel create tunalen-main. This generated a unique UUID and a corresponding credential file (<UUID>.json).
10.2. Tunnel Configuration File
The tunnel's behavior is defined in /etc/cloudflared/config.yml. This file instructs cloudflared on how to route incoming public traffic.
# /etc/cloudflared/config.yml

# This must match the name of the tunnel created in the previous step
tunnel: tunalen-main

# Defines how incoming public traffic is routed to internal services
ingress:
  # Rule 1: Handles requests for the primary application domain
  - hostname: www.tunalen.com
    # Forwards traffic to the Streamlit container on its exposed port
    service: http://localhost:8501

  # Rule 2: A CNAME record for the root domain also points to this tunnel
  - hostname: tunalen.com
    service: http://localhost:8501

  # Rule 3: Catch-all. Any request not matching a hostname above gets a 404 error.
  - service: http_status:404
Use code with caution.
Yaml
10.3. DNS and Service Automation
DNS Routing: The public hostnames were linked to the tunnel using the command: cloudflared tunnel route dns tunalen-main www.tunalen.com. This automatically created the necessary CNAME records in the Cloudflare DNS dashboard. A manual CNAME record was created for the root domain (@) pointing to www.tunalen.com.
Service Automation: The tunnel is managed by a systemd service located at /etc/systemd/system/cloudflared.service. This ensures the tunnel starts automatically when the WSL instance boots and restarts on failure.
11. Security Configuration
Cloudflare Access Policy: An application was created in the Cloudflare Zero Trust dashboard for www.tunalen.com. An Access Policy is enforced that requires users to authenticate with an email address from a pre-approved list before any traffic is allowed to pass through the tunnel.
robots.txt Transform Rule: A Cloudflare Transform Rule was created to intercept any request for /robots.txt and reply directly from the Cloudflare edge with a "Disallow All" message, preventing reputable search engines from indexing the site.
12. Verification and Maintenance
Service Status: The tunnel's operational status can be verified with sudo systemctl status cloudflared.
Live Logs: Real-time logs can be viewed for troubleshooting with sudo journalctl -u cloudflared -f.
Persistence: The service runs independently of any open terminal windows and persists through reboots of the WSL instance or the Windows host machine
